## Phase 8 — one per-worker analytics view over skillRuns

New src/lib/worker-analytics.ts generalizes package-overview.ts's
per-skill rollup (Showtime-only, keyed off SKILL_IDS) to the full
unified worker registry across both products:
- getWorkspaceWorkerOverview(whopUserId, workspaceId): one row per
  worker in WORKER_IDS, workspace-scoped (not just whopUserId, unlike
  package-overview.ts — the point of this whole effort is that
  workspaceId is now the real client boundary).
- getWorkerAnalyticsDetail(whopUserId, workspaceId, workerId): the
  per-worker drill-down package-overview.ts never needed — recent runs,
  success rate, needs-attention streak, all scoped to one workerId.

New /dashboard/analytics/[workerId] page — the literal "one per-worker
view parameterized by workerId" the roadmap named for this phase.
worker-card.tsx's Analytics button now points every worker (either
product) at this one destination shape, replacing the old
PRODUCT_ANALYTICS_HREF lookup that sent Showtime workers to one page
and Reputation Manager workers to a completely different one.

/dashboard/reputation-manager/analytics — the standalone RM analytics
page (4 stat cards + sentiment bar over rep_engine_findings/
rep_trustpilot_reviews/rep_reddit_mentions, no skillRuns data at all) —
is now a redirect into /dashboard/analytics/rep-onboarding. Nothing
lost: that exact same rollup (getWorkerAnalyticsDetail's repSignals)
now renders on every RM worker's own analytics page instead of one
page nothing else in the app linked to.

Deliberately left untouched: Showtime's existing /dashboard/analytics
overview (win-back funnel, show-rate calibration, Pile-On mix,
cross-client benchmark, booking-sync distribution) — a real, working,
1000+-line page with legitimate Showtime-specific depth, not divergent
duplicate query logic to retire. Folding it into the unified registry
is a distinct, larger follow-up, not risked in this pass.

## Phase 9 — generative dashboard panels

New ActiveWorkersPanel on the main dashboard: one card per worker
actually enabled for the workspace's client (via Phase 3's
getEnabledWorkerIdsForEngagement), each showing its weekly run count,
success rate, and a needs-attention flag — sourced from Phase 8's
getWorkspaceWorkerOverview so the two phases share one query instead
of the dashboard adding its own. Needs-attention workers sort first.
Nothing new to navigate to: each card links to the worker's own
Phase 8 analytics page and its existing configure/bridges page.

